  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1155 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nTuesday through Sunday:\n\nWith a low pressure to the northwest in Manitoba moving\nnortheast toward the James Bay Wednesday into Thursday, a cold\nfront is expected to cross through Wisconsin. As this front\nmoves through this looks to be our best chance for rain so far.\nThe front is expected to pass through southern Wisconsin\nWednesday night into Thursday morning bringing some light rain\nand small chance for a couple rumbles of thunder. Models\ncontinue to show a chance for some elevated CAPE which is\npossible with the persistent mid level WAA out ahead of the\nfront. Southern Wisconsin will be in the warm sector all day\nout ahead of the front as well to prime the environment.\nThankfully the time of day that the front moves through is less\nthan ideal. So while thunder is on the table, nothing else is.\nBehind the front, cooler temperatures will briefly move in\nknocking Thursday night lows into the lower 40s to lower 50s\n(similar to tonight). \n\nHigh pressure and another upper level ridge will briefly move in\nbehind the cold front for Thursday into Friday, but then some\nuncertainty returns to the forecast. This high pressure at the\nsfc looks to be fairly weak while an upper level trough ejects\nout of the desert southwest undercutting the ridging. The trough\nand subsequent short waves paths vary model to model. This \npaints a fairly broad area with upper level PVA and chance for \nrain. The current average among guidance for POPS is 30-60% \nfrom roughly Friday evening through Monday. The exact path of \nthe shortwaves and trough, the amount of available moisture and \npotentially WAA will play a roll in how much rain we get over \nthis period. The take away here is there are chances for rain, \nwe are entering into a wetter pattern/period, but this isnt \nexpected to be a washout. The timing of rain should tighten up \nas models gain some more certainty/consensus. \n\nPatterson",
